<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Astrakhan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1466 CE – 1556 CE (approx.) · capital Xacitarxan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tatar khanate at the mouth of the Volga, a successor of the Golden Horde whose annexation in 1556 gave Russia the whole Volga route.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Astrakhan Khanate formed in the 1460s around the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Xacitarxan</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> delta, under khans descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuchuk-muhammad">Küchük Muhammad</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-horde">Great Horde</a>. It became fully independent after the Great Horde's destruction in 1502.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khanate was small and relatively weak, controlling the fisheries and trade of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> towards the Caspian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Its population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> lived largely by herding and trade. Its khans were frequently dependent on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nogai-horde">Nogai Horde</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a>, and several were installed by these neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> turned south: Russian forces installed a client khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dervish-ali">Dervish Ali</a>, in 1554 and annexed the khanate in 1556. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> became a Russian fortress city and gateway to the Caspian and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/astrakhan-khanate.jpg" alt="Astrakhan Khanate" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Untifler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astrakhan_Khanate_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1466 CE – 1556 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xacitarxan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qasim I, Dervish Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Astrakhan Tatar, Nogai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Astrakhan, Xacitarxan Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=astrakhan-khanate" aria-label="Astrakhan Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5810 544 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Astrakhan Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6130 800L6352 637M6130 800L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6130 800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6130 800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Xacitarxan. <a href="/atlas?year=1511&amp;focus=astrakhan-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
